===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|accorden}}, {{m|enm|acorden}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|acorder}} (compare modern French {{m|fr|accord}} and {{m|en|accorder}}), from {{der|en|VL.|*accordāre}}, from {{der|en|la|concordo|concordāre}} via prefix substitution (with {{der|en|la|ad-}}), ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|[[*ḱḗr]] ~ [[*ḱr̥d-]]}}, and thus distantly related to {{cog|en|heart}} (via {{cog|gem-pro|*hertô}}).

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make to agree or [[correspond]]; to suit one thing to another; to [[adjust]].
#* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|chapter=11|folio=150|passage=[H]er hands '''accorded''' the Lutes muſicke to the voice; {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring (people) to an agreement; to [[reconcile]], settle, adjust or [[harmonize]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1590|book=III|canto=IX|page=532|passage=But ''Satyrane'' forth ſtepping, did them ſtay / And with faire treaty pacifide their yre; / Then when they were '''accorded''' from the fray, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:South Twelve Sermons|volume=IV|chapter=A Sermon Preached at St. Mary’s Church in Oxon, before the University,{{nb...|on the 29th of July 1660, Being the Time of the King’s Commissioners Meeting There, Soon after the Restauration, for the Visitation of That University.}}|date=29 July 1660|pages=17–18|pageref=18|passage=All which Particulars (with many more of the like Nature) being confeſſedly knotty and difficult, can never be '''accorded''', but by a competent Stock of critical Learning; {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To agree or [[correspond]]; to be in harmony;  to be [[concordant#Adjective|concordant]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|act=III|scene=i|page=132|column=2|passage=For things are often ſpoke, and ſeldome meant, / But that my heart '''accordeth''' with my tongue, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|book=3|page=54|lines=9–11|passage=Thy actions to thy words '''accord''', thy words / To thy large heart give utterance due, thy heart / Conteins of good, wiſe, juſt, the perfect ſhape.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|part=Prologue|page=vi|passage=Let knowledge grow from more to more, / ⁠But more of reverence in us dwell; / ⁠That mind and soul, '''according''' well, / May make one music as before, / But vaster.}}
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=The Gateway, and Some Who Passed|page=29|passage=Carried somehow, somewhither, for some reason, on these surging floods, were these travelers,{{nb...}}. Even such a boat as the ''Mount Vernon'' offered a total deck space so cramped as to leave secrecy or privacy well out of the question, even had the motley and democratic assemblage of passengers been disposed to '''accord''' either.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To agree in pitch and tone.
# {{lb|en|transitive|legal}} To [[grant]] as suitable or proper; to [[concede]] or [[award]].
#* {{RQ:Shelley Poetical Works|I|The Revolt of Islam|217|And, when the blinding tears had fallen, I saw<br>That column, and those corpses, and the moon,<br>And felt the poisonous tooth of hunger gnaw<br>My vitals, I rejoiced, as if the boon<br>Of senseless death would be '''accorded''' soon;— {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1951|author=United Nations|title=[[s:1951 Refugee Convention|Convention Relating to the Status of Refugees]]|section=article 14|passage=In respect of the protection of industrial property,{{...}}a refugee shall be '''accorded''' in the country in which he has his habitual residence the same protection as is '''accorded''' to nationals of that country.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1952|month=January|author=Henry Maxwell|title=Farewell to the "T14s"|journal=Railway Magazine|page=57|text=Yes, the tide will surely turn, and meanwhile may one who is proud to call himself a partisan, invite whomever may feel disposed to bid the "T14s" adieux, to pause before giving them valediction and '''accord''' to them the respect that is assuredly their due.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=European Court of Human Rights|title=A, B and C v. Ireland|section=marginal 235|date=16-12-2010|issue=25579/05|url=http://hudoc.echr.coe.int/eng?i=001-102332|passage=In the present case, and contrary to the Government’s submission, the Court considers that there is indeed a consensus amongst a substantial majority of the Contracting States of the Council of Europe towards allowing abortion on broader grounds than '''accorded''' under Irish law.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To give consent.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic}} To arrive at an agreement.

